So some historical hashes are "unresolved" because the files were overwritten — that's expected but must be distinguished from "never existed"/spurious. A better classification:
- `bound_current`: cited hash matches the current on-disk sha256 of the review's declared target_path (the gate-relevant case).
- `matched_elsewhere`: cited prefix matches some other on-disk file (mis-binding — serious).
- `historical_unresolved`: cited prefix doesn't match any on-disk artifact (revision overwritten; verdict cannot be re-verified against current artifact — informational, expected under overwrite workflow).
- `no_binding_field`: review has no artifact_sha256/reviewed_sha256/dispatched_sha256 (gate cannot cite it).

